00:00:01.213 --> 00:00:02.880
POLONG LIN: In this
video, I'll show you

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.610
how to build and
deploy a recommendation

00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.220
system with BigQuery ML.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.830
My name is Polong Lin and
I'm a developer advocate

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.660
for Google Cloud.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.820
From finding the right
Halloween costume,

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.560
getting notified
of relevant spooky

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.410
decorations for your home,
the majority of consumers

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:22.830
today expect personalization,
recommendations

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:25.380
of products and
services, or content

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:27.330
tailored to their interests.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:30.150
And so, in order to better
serve the customers,

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:33.660
businesses need to be able to
train and deploy recommendation

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.280
systems quickly and effectively
to improve conversions,

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.760
click-through rates, and build
customer satisfaction, loyalty,

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.290
and brand affinity.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.630
But to build
recommendation systems,

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.130
you need a place for your data.

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:51.360
Google BigQuery is a fully
managed, highly scalable data

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.640
warehouse solution
on Google Cloud.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.630
It comes with lots
of great features,

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:58.620
especially around
storage and analytics.

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.230
Without needing to move
your data out of BigQuery,

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:04.200
with BigQuery ML, you can
train and deploy machine

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.380
learning models
directly using SQL.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.980
That means you've got data
storage, analytics, and machine

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.410
learning all within BigQuery.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:17.040
So how can businesses create
recommendation systems quickly

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:20.310
and deploy them into
their online stores?

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.700
In this video, I'll show you
how to prepare your training

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.520
data in BigQuery,
train a recommendation

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:30.390
system with BigQuery ML, use
the predicted recommendations

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:34.900
in production, and tips
and best practices.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:37.710
Let's start with the data.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:39.840
To recommend
something to someone,

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.120
you kind of need to know
what they like already.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.540
So how do you know
what a user likes?

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:48.240
In general, there are two ways.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.990
If your users directly give
you ratings of 4 and 1/2

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.340
stars out of 5 on a particular
product or piece of content,

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.440
then this is called
explicit feedback.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:03.090
The second way is to
infer interest indirectly.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.400
For example, maybe
the more time a user

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.600
spends viewing a product, the
more they're interested in it.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.720
Since you're inferring interest
indirectly or implicitly,

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.780
this is called
implicit feedback.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.550
As implicit feedback
tends to be more

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.680
common in retail
businesses, let's

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.230
build a recommendation
system using some sample

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.860
implicit feedback data
from a real online store.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.520
The Google Merchandise Store
is an actual e-commerce store

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.920
where people can shop for
Google-branded shirts, hats,

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:34.380
and other products.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:37.500
And a sample of the anonymized
Google Analytics data

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.430
is available as one of the
BigQuery public data sets.

00:02:41.430 --> 00:02:44.040
Based on the store's
Google Analytics data,

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.640
I've cleaned the data so that we
can see each anonymized visitor

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.090
ID, what product
they were looking at,

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.370
and how long they spent
viewing the product, which

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.050
is in milliseconds here.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:58.800
We need these three columns to
train a recommendation system

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.770
in BigQuery ML--

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:04.330
the user, the product,
and the ratings.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.610
So the visitor in the
first row, for example,

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:10.140
viewed a particular item
for 59 milliseconds.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:12.600
And in the second row,
a different visitor

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:19.450
looked at this item for 5,787
milliseconds, and so on.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:22.860
So essentially, the more time
they spend viewing the product,

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:25.960
the higher the implicit rating.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:29.060
With the data ready, to trade
the recommendation system,

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:32.110
we'll be using the matrix
factorization algorithm

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:33.880
in BigQuery ML.

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.790
So selecting the data
is straightforward here.

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:39.880
Select star from
the view or table.

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:43.330
With the training data, we
can now create the model

00:03:43.330 --> 00:03:46.600
by specifying a model name
like retail recommender

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:49.480
in our data set BQML.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.150
Then we specify the options.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:55.720
Model type, which in this
case is matrix factorization

00:03:55.720 --> 00:04:00.040
for recommendation systems, the
user column, the item column,

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.720
and the rating column.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.120
I'm setting feedback
type to implicit

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.040
because the model is
trained differently

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.280
for explicit and
implicit feedback.

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.980
And then within AS, we
select the training data.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:15.040
Now as you execute
this query, if you

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.430
get an error that says training
matrix factorization models is

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:21.220
not available for
on-demand usage,

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:23.080
it's because matrix
factorization can

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:25.650
be quite computationally
expensive,

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.730
so BigQuery it's
trying to protect you

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:29.950
from a potentially hefty bill.

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:33.220
To rectify this, you'll need
to use flat-rate pricing.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:36.040
And to learn more about this,
follow the guide in the link

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.160
here.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.740
After training is done,
you can evaluate your model

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:44.310
using select star
from ML dot evaluate.

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:45.870
But you might be
wondering what data

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.420
it's evaluating the model on.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:51.300
When you create a model,
BigQuery ML automatically

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:54.750
splits the training data into
its own training and testing

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:57.100
data behind the scenes for you.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:00.450
So this allows you to evaluate
your model immediately

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.820
after the model has
finished training.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.030
Now of these evaluation
metrics, average rank,

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.070
also known as mean
percentile rank,

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:11.460
is perhaps the most widely
used metric for implicit matrix

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:12.960
factorization.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.250
In general, the lower
the average rank,

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:17.340
the more closely the
predicted recommendations

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.040
match the behavior
in the test set,

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:25.870
with 0.5 being random chance and
0 being a perfect prediction.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:28.180
If you're happy with
the evaluation metrics,

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.070
you can now make predictions.

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:33.340
To make predictions with
BigQuery ML on a single user

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:36.160
or a set of users to start,
you can run select star

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.530
from ML dot
recommend, specifying

00:05:38.530 --> 00:05:42.610
the model you created,
and the user ID or IDs

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:45.090
that you want to
make predictions for.

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.560
To get the top N
recommendations,

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:51.370
you can order by the predicted
confidence descending

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:57.920
and limit N. You can also make
predictions on all the users

00:05:57.920 --> 00:05:59.710
so you have the top
N recommendations

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:01.750
for every single user.

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:04.660
For example, here we're looking
at the top five recommendations

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:06.750
for all the users.

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:09.930
Note that because the output
for predicting the top N

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:12.660
recommendations for
every single user

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:14.550
can actually get quite large.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:17.790
And because we may want to use
the results multiple times,

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:21.330
it may be better to store the
outputs in a separate table

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:24.960
that you can reference more
easily later, like here, prod

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:28.890
recommendations under
the BQML data set.

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.500
Now that we have our
predicted recommendations,

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:35.310
what can we do to use the
recommendations in production?

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:37.590
One way is to export
recommendations

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:42.070
for add retargeting campaigns
with Google Analytics.

00:06:42.070 --> 00:06:46.210
First, by focusing on
specific products at a time,

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:49.030
you can create a new
column for likelihood

00:06:49.030 --> 00:06:51.190
to purchase this
product based on

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:53.230
the predicted recommendations.

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:56.260
Then you can import
your predictions back

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:59.260
into Google Analytics
to create new campaigns

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.070
for those products.

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:04.300
Another way is by connecting
the predicted recommendations

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:07.210
with your CRM, like Salesforce.

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:10.420
And by doing so, you can
create targeted email campaigns

00:07:10.420 --> 00:07:12.640
to provide relevant
products directly

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:14.710
to your customers' inboxes.

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:17.290
There are even integrations
between Google Analytics

00:07:17.290 --> 00:07:21.970
and Salesforce that
you can leverage.

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:24.310
You can also export
the recommendations

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:27.490
to Cloud Storage, a
separate database,

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:31.060
or simply read from BigQuery
to use in a separate service

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:34.260
to serve the recommendations.

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:37.710
Now, at this point, I want
to cover some tips and best

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:40.120
practices, as you
might be thinking

00:07:40.120 --> 00:07:42.660
how you can potentially
improve your recommendation

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:44.880
systems in production.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:49.180
One question you might have
is, what happens to new users?

00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:52.290
In other words, since matrix
factorization can only

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:56.590
make predictions on users who
belong to the training data,

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:58.440
how do you make
predictions on users who

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.630
you don't have any data for?

00:08:00.630 --> 00:08:02.400
Well, this is actually
a common problem

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.380
called the cold-start problem.

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:06.250
There are many ways
to deal with this,

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:08.770
which is outside the
scope of this video.

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:11.100
But as a simple
suggestion the easiest way

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:15.402
would be to show users items
that are sorted by popularity

00:08:15.402 --> 00:08:16.860
or what products
have been trending

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:19.380
for the past timed interval.

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:22.200
Another question you may ask
is, how do you recommend items

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:26.340
to new users if you know some
of their demographic data?

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:30.000
One way is to find
lookalikes to those new users

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.059
based on your pool
of existing users.

00:08:33.059 --> 00:08:38.299
For example, given some
demographic data for User X,

00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:42.740
first compare User X to
all of the existing users

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:46.440
that you already have
recommendations for.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:49.710
Then find the five
most similar users,

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:54.630
we'll call these lookalikes,
to User X based on whatever

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:56.040
data you may have--

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:58.850
demographic data or otherwise.

00:08:58.850 --> 00:09:03.020
Then make
recommendations to User X

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:06.830
based on what these
lookalikes would themselves

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:08.540
be recommended.

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:10.880
To find lookalikes,
one way is to use

00:09:10.880 --> 00:09:15.680
cosine similarity between
User X and the other users.

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:17.900
Another way that you
can find lookalikes

00:09:17.900 --> 00:09:20.150
is to find the nearest
cluster of users

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:22.610
and use them as your lookalikes.

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:25.220
And you can do this using
a clustering algorithm

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:29.850
like k-means, which is also
available with BigQuery ML.

00:09:29.850 --> 00:09:33.340
For example, given
four clusters of users,

00:09:33.340 --> 00:09:35.820
we may find that
User X is closest

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:39.210
in distance to the centroid
of these yellow squared data

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:40.380
points.

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:43.500
And so we can recommend
to User X products

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.870
that a user in this
cluster would themselves

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:49.110
be recommended.

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:51.970
Another question you may
have is that BigQuery ML does

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:54.690
batch predictions very
well, but your business

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:58.080
may need predictions on
a single user at a time,

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:00.750
and you need the
predictions to be fast.

00:10:00.750 --> 00:10:03.030
In these situations,
you can export

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:06.600
your matrix factorization
models from BigQuery ML

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:09.600
and host it for real-time
predictions using

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:12.000
AI Platform on Google Cloud.

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.250
To learn more about how
to host these models

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:18.790
for real-time predictions,
you can follow this link.

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:20.970
So now you should know
how to build and deploy

00:10:20.970 --> 00:10:24.247
a recommendation system
with BigQuery ML.

00:10:24.247 --> 00:10:25.080
Thanks for watching.

00:10:25.080 --> 00:10:28.430
